    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The church is monotheistic: members believe in a supreme being, the Great Spirit or Creator, and use peyote as a sacrament that they hold to be a means of communicating with the Great Spirit.</li><li>It has two main ways, the Half-Moon way and the Cross-fire way, which differ in whether a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, baptism and a minister are used.</li><li>Its use of peyote came under federal drug law in 1970 and was later protected by the American Indian Religious Freedom Act of 1978 and the Religious Freedom Restoration Act of 1993.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ceremonies are held at the request of a family, usually last all night, and end with a feast for the whole community and gifts from the sponsoring family to the Roadman and his helpers.</p></aside>
    </section>

