    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Native American Church, also known as Peyotism and Peyote Religion, is a syncretic Native American religion that combines traditional Native American beliefs with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, especially some of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a>, and the sacramental use of the entheogen peyote. It originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> Territory (1890–1907) in the late nineteenth century, after peyote was introduced to the southern Great Plains from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>Many denominations of mainstream Christianity had tried to convert Native Americans in the Western Hemisphere. These efforts were partly successful, and the religious practices of many Native American tribes, those of the Native American Church included, reflect Christian creeds. Conversion to Christianity was a slow process, but the tenets of the Native American Church were more readily accepted. The church included Protestant characteristics in its formation.</p><p>The church has more than one date for its founding as an institution. Victor Griffin, known as the last chief of the Quapaw tribe, is noted for facilitating its incorporation under Oklahoma law in 1911; the original charter of the Native American Church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> dates from 1913; and the church was officially created in 1918. Early in the twentieth century anthropologists led by James Mooney testified before legislative bodies on behalf of Peyotists and encouraged the movement's leaders to consolidate peyote use into an established religion whose practice would be protected by law.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Native American Church is monotheistic, believing in a supreme being called the Great Spirit, also referred to as the Creator or God. Its tenets regard peyote as a sacred and holy sacrament and use it as a means to communicate with the Great Spirit. Members also believe in the Trinity of God, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the Holy Ghost, which in Half-Moon rituals is represented by the three leaders present.</p><p>Members believe that disease and death result from an imbalance in the individual. Besides peyote, other sacred plants, prayer and fasting are used to cure this imbalance. Peyote is never used for recreation, and its hallucinogenic effects are considered spiritual visions; to most Native Americans, visions are a communion with the metaphysical. Not every member experiences hallucinogenic effects during peyote rituals. The plant is meant to heal or fix social, personal and communal problems, and members believe it is safe for children and pregnant women.</p><p>Services are held on special request by a family, whether to celebrate a birthday, hold a memorial or funeral, cure illness, mark a school graduation or another significant life event, or keep a Christian holiday such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. A participant sits up all night, giving up a full night's rest as a small sacrifice to the Holy Spirit and Jesus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The church has no scripture of its own. Its ceremonies include scripture reading, prayer, singing and drumming, and the use of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> differs between its ways. In the Cross-fire way a Bible is placed on an altar and Bible sections are recited according to the family's prayer for the particular service; among the Lakota, Cross-fire groups use the Bible for sermons, which Half-Moon followers reject, although both teach a similar Christian morality.</p><p>Music is central to a service. The singer holds a gourd rattle staff and is joined by a water-drummer with a water drum; the singer sings four songs, concludes the set, and passes the staff, gourd and drum to the next relative. Only these two instruments are used in an authentic prayer service. The songs are in what is called the peyote language, which parallels words in the Coahuilteco and Comecrudo languages of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. It was introduced by the Carrizo and Coahuiltecan people of South Texas, and it makes up the songs sung around all fireplaces, whatever the tribe or denomination; these are the songs people consider "straight". Many tribes also work their own language into peyote songs, which ties a song to one tribe rather than leaving it intertribal.</p><p>Among the Menominee, prayers were said in Menomini and ended with "In the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost. Amen", taken from the Trinitarian baptismal formula. N. Scott Momaday gives a portrayal of the peyote service in his novel House Made of Dawn.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The church is today the most widespread indigenous religion among Native Americans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> (except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> Natives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a>), in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, among First Nations people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberta">Alberta</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with an estimated 300,000 adherents.</p><p>It has several ways, or traditions. The two main ones are the Half-Moon way and the Cross-fire way. The Half-Moon way was introduced in 1914 or 1924 by James Seymour, who was a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chunk">Winnebago</a> tribe. Among the Menominee, Peyotism is described as unique, with Catholic beliefs and practices incorporated after years of Catholic missionaries, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> has also had an influence. Many Cross-fire adherents, such as the Dakota Cross-fire and Winnebago Cross-fire members, strongly oppose the use of tobacco and smoking in rituals and ceremonies. Prominent figures in the church's development include Chevato, Jim Aton, John Wilson and Jonathan Koshiway, and Victor Griffin helped spread the religion to some other related tribes in the region.</p><p>The fireplaces, the ceremonial peyote altars passed down in families, vary greatly with tribe and environment, and some special fireplaces fit neither main style. The Half-Moon fireplace uses a half-moon shaped sand altar and tobacco and corn-shucks, and its staff is passed around the tipi. The Cross-Fire fireplace uses a horseshoe shaped sand altar with a mound outside the tipi to represent the "grave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a>", no tobacco, and a staff placed upright in the ground.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ceremonies are generally held in a tipi and must be conducted by a priest, pastor or elder known as the Roadman. He is assisted by a Fireman, who tends the holy fireplace so that it burns steadily all night. The Roadman may use a prayer staff, a beaded and feathered gourd, a small drum, cedar and his eagle feather. Services commonly begin at sundown on a Friday or Saturday evening and end at sunrise. Very early, between 4:30 and 5:00 in the morning, the Roadman's wife or another female relative prepares four sacramental foods: water, shredded beef or "sweet meat", corn mush and a variety of berries. A second breakfast is served well after sunrise, just before the service closes.</p><p>The service is followed by a feast for the whole community. The sponsoring family then gives gifts to the Roadman and all his helpers to show deep appreciation for their work.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> there are two main umbrella chapters. The Native American Church of the United States holds the original 1913 charter, and its chapters have no blood quantum requirement to attend ceremonies. The Native American Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, an offshoot from the late 1960s, admits only Native Americans with a blood quantum of one quarter or more, checked through Certificates of Degree of Indian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> Native Blood and church membership cards. It is in the minority nationally. Both have tribal offshoots, notably among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government became more involved in controlling drugs, the church faced possible legal difficulties over peyote. The Controlled Substances Act, signed by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> in 1970, prohibited possession of psychedelics such as peyote and classified them as controlled drugs. The American Indian Religious Freedom Act of 1978 was passed to protect the church's use of the plant, and federal rulings such as US v. Boyll (1991) and the Religious Freedom Restoration Act of 1993 allow members to transport, possess and use peyote for religious purposes.</p><p>Such use has been declared legal without regard to race or tribal status in all states other than Idaho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, which have outlawed use by non-natives. Peyote can be bought from licensed distributors in Texas, who sell wild peyote gathered by licensed collectors, only by enrolled tribal members with a permit and the intent to use it for religious purposes. The Neo-American Church tried to claim LSD and marijuana as sacraments, and the courts ruled against it. Reuben Snake, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chunk">Ho-Chunk</a> roadman, worked towards the American Indian Religious Freedom Act.</p><p>The church also left a body of art: gourd rattles, feather fans, ritual staffs and drum sticks carved with tipis, birds, stars and sun patterns, made with European trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and metal as well as traditional materials, along with paintings and jewellery.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Native American Church is a syncretic Native American religion that joins traditional beliefs with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the sacramental use of peyote. It arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> Territory in the late nineteenth century and has an estimated 300,000 adherents in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Its legal protection for peyote came through federal laws of 1978 and 1993.</em></p>

